  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 227 PM EST FRI NOV 13 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, 500 mb troughing will be \ncentered over New England, with ridging over the Midwest. \nMeanwhile, a cutoff low will be progressing through the Southern \nPlains, with additional broader troughing centered over the \nPacific Coast. At the surface, high pressure will be located over \nthe Midwest, with no other notable features near eastern Kentucky.\nThis area of high pressure will be the dominant player in the \nlong term forecast, maintaining tranquil and uneventful weather \nthrough the entire long term period.\n\nInitially, some cloud cover will linger on Sunday as winds remain\nnorthwesterly and just enough low level moisture will support \nupslope clouds, particularly in southeastern Kentucky. This \npattern will keep high temperatures cool on Sunday, likely in the \nupper 40s to low 50s across the area. As ridging builds east, \nhowever, the region of surface high pressure will progress \neastward as well, marking a shift in surface winds from \nnorthwesterly to northeasterly late Sunday into early Monday. Any \ncloud cover should dissipate, and a weak pressure gradient \nassociated with this high should allow for ideal radiative cooling\nconditions on Sunday night and support the development of a \nridge/valley split. Valley temperatures could plummet into the mid\n20s, while ridges will likely stay warmer in the 30s. However, if\ncloud cover lingers longer than expected, lows could end up being\nwarmer than forecast in these locations.\n\nBy Monday, clear skies will set in, and as upper level ridging \ncontinues to build into our area, attendant high pressure at the \nsurface should limit cloud cover through Wednesday. Even as the \naforementioned cutoff low progresses east, this system will begin \nto dissipate along the Gulf Coast by Tuesday before being absorbed\ninto broader troughing along the Atlantic Coast, with no impact \nto the forecast area. Instead, midlevel heights should rise \nthrough Wednesday, and by midweek, LREF mean 500 mb heights look \nto be anomalously high, roughly the 95th percentile compared to \nclimatology. At the surface, this will allow temperatures to warm \neach day, and by midweek the surface high will be east of the area\nwith winds becoming more southwesterly, advecting warmer and more\nmoist air into eastern Kentucky. Model spread does increase \nsomewhat by Thursday as the main ridge axis shifts east of the \nforecast area; however, confidence in above average temperatures \nremains high, with ensemble guidance suggesting high temperatures \nare likely to remain in at least the mid 60s, even as increased \nmoisture could allow a few clouds to return to the forecast. Lows \nshould also follow this warming trend. Although mostly clear skies\novernight will allow valleys to decouple, setting the stage for \nridge/valley splits each night, higher dewpoints will keep \novernight lows noticeably milder for all locations by the end of \nthe forecast period. Even so, no precipitation is expected through\nFriday.",
